<h2 id="get-started">Start your Sacramento side hustle in 6 steps</h2>
<ol class="steps">
  <li><strong>Choose by schedule, not hype.</strong> State workers with fixed 8-to-5 hours do well with evening delivery or weekend services; shift workers can take weekday daytime gigs when competition is thin.</li>
  <li><strong>Pick a territory.</strong> Focus on one area — say Natomas or Elk Grove — so drive time doesn't eat your profit.</li>
  <li><strong>Handle the paperwork.</strong> Inside city limits, get a Business Operations Tax certificate and, if you work from home, a home occupation permit. Suburbs have their own rules.</li>
  <li><strong>Get visible locally.</strong> Set up a Google Business Profile and post in Nextdoor and neighborhood Facebook groups.</li>
  <li><strong>Collect reviews early.</strong> Your first five reviews do more than any ad.</li>
  <li><strong>Separate your money.</strong> Open a separate checking account and track mileage from day one. <a href="/tools/self-employment-tax-calculator/">Estimate your self-employment tax</a> so April isn't a surprise.</li>
</ol>

<h2 id="california-rules">California taxes and rules for side hustlers</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>State income tax:</strong> California taxes side income at your regular rate, from 1% up to 12.3% (plus a 1% surcharge over $1 million). Add federal income tax and 15.3% self-employment tax on net profit. Setting aside 25–35% is a safe default.</li>
  <li><strong>AB5 and contractor status:</strong> California uses the "ABC test" to decide whether a worker is an employee. If a Sacramento business hires you for ongoing work that's part of its core business, it may be required to put you on payroll. Many freelance professions have exemptions, but know the rules before you sign a long-term contract.</li>
  <li><strong>Prop 22:</strong> app-based rideshare and delivery drivers are contractors with a guaranteed earnings floor for engaged time and some health-care stipends at higher hour levels.</li>
  <li><strong>Sales tax:</strong> if you sell physical products, register for a seller's permit with the CDTFA.</li>
  <li><strong>Local permits:</strong> the City of Sacramento requires a BOT certificate for businesses operating in the city; food sales need county health permits.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout callout-warn"><strong>Watch out:</strong> California doesn't follow every federal deduction. Keep clean records and consider a tax pro once your side income passes a few thousand dollars a year.</div>

